== A Servant’s Faithful and Willing Service

=== §. 20. #emph[Of serving with trembling.]

The #emph[manner] in which servants ought to perform their duties is noted in four phrases:

The first of them is this, #emph[with fear and trembling.] #footnote[I.]

#emph[Fear] is both a #emph[fountain] from which all other duties flow, and a #emph[sauce] to season them all.

Commonly the taste and savor of waters come from the fountain. Saint James implies this where he says that #emph[no fountain yields salt water and fresh:]#footnote[Jas 3:12.] for if the fountain is salt, the streams flowing from it will be salt; and fresh, if the fountain is fresh. So if fear is seated in the heart of servants, all their obedience and submission will be seasoned with it. Let servants therefore learn here to show, by their manner of performing all their duties, that there is a true servant-like fear seated in their hearts. On this I need say no more than what has been #footnote[§. 4.] said before.

The other word, #footnote[See Treatise 1, §. 124.] #emph[trembling,] added to fear, adds emphasis, showing that it is no small fear that is required of servants. It also gives them to understand that, since their masters have power to punish them, they must conduct themselves so that they do not provoke their master to wrath, but be very careful and watchful to avoid his displeasure, so that they give him no just cause of offense. This care was in that servant of David who first spotted Absalom hanging in a tree and told Joab of it: #footnote[2 Sam 18:12.] he so feared the displeasure of the king his master that he dared not kill Absalom, even to gain a thousand shekels of silver. The same is noted of Obadiah, who was afraid to tell his master Ahab where Elijah was, for fear that his master would think he had mocked him, if the Spirit carried Elijah away. #footnote[1 Kgs 18:9. #emph[etc.]]

This trembling fear is needful because of the little love that servants commonly bear to their masters. There are not the same motives to stir up love in servants toward their masters as in children toward their parents. Unless, therefore, they are kept within bounds through awe and dread, they will transgress exceedingly. And because this is so needful, servants must labor to nourish it, as a means to keep them from too much boldness.

Contrary to this, on the one side, is a proud despising of a master’s authority (saying, if not with their mouths, yet in their hearts, as Gaal said of Abimelech, “Who is Abimelech … that we should serve him?”#footnote[Judg 9:28.] or as those who despised the government of Christ their master, “We do not want this man to rule over us”;#footnote[Luke 19:14.] and again, “Let us break His chains and cast away His cords”).#footnote[Ps 2:3.] And on the other side, a wretched carelessness, not fearing any punishment before they feel it, like many reckless thieves who do not fear the power of the judge in the least, but recklessly say, #emph[We have but one death to pay.] The authority of God himself is despised, and his avenging hand is lightly regarded, by such proud and reckless servants; so their sin is no small sin.

=== §. 21. #emph[Of serving with sincerity.]

The second branch concerning the #emph[manner] of servants’ performing their duty is in these words, #emph[in singleness of heart,] #footnote[II.] so that everything must be performed with an honest and upright heart. “Whatever you#super[+] do, do it heartily,” says the apostle to servants #footnote[Col 3:23.] in another place. In this way Joseph served his master in singleness of heart: witness his refusing to lie with his mistress in #footnote[Gen 39:11, 12.] a private room when she desired it and no one else was in the house. Happy would it be for masters to have such servants! Then they might take no more care than Potiphar did,#footnote[Gen 39:6.] but put all that they have into their servants’ hands. Nor would this rare virtue in servants be profitable only to their masters; it would also be a great comfort to the servants themselves, and bring them much peace of conscience.

Contrary to this is hypocritical service: when servants have #emph[a heart and a heart,] making a show of one heart outwardly and having another, even a completely contrary heart, within them.#footnote[Ps 12:2.] Such a one was Gehazi, who “went in and stood before his master” as if he had done his master some good service, when #footnote[2 Kgs 5:25.] he had most highly dishonored him. And such a one was Judas, who showed as fair a face to his master as any of the disciples, and yet was an arrant traitor: for when he was thought to be going out to buy provisions for his master, he went to #footnote[John 13:29.] betray him.

All eye-service is contrary to the singleness of heart named above: when servants are diligent so long as their master’s eye is on them, #footnote[Eye-service.] like little children who will do anything their mother wants them to do while her eye is on them, but nothing when her back is turned. The world is full of such eye-serving servants, who, while their masters are present, will be as busy as bees; but if the master is away, they are either idling at home, or roaming about outside, or doing nothing but quarreling, and eating, and drinking with drunkards, like that wicked servant whom Christ notes in the parable.#footnote[Matt 24:49.] Let such servants take note of the judgment denounced against him.

=== §. 22. #emph[Of serving for conscience’ sake.]

The third branch of the #emph[manner] of servants’ performing #footnote[III. #emph[Sit seruus non vt seruus ad oculum, sed vt domini studiosus. Constit. Apost. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 12. \[Let a servant be, not a servant to the eye, but one devoted to his master.\]] their duty is in these words, #emph[as to Christ, as the servants of Christ, doing the will of God, as to the Lord.] All of these set forth a good conscience, or such service as is performed #emph[for conscience’ sake,] or #emph[for the Lord’s sake,] which is all one, because the conscience has an eye only to the Lord, to his will and to his ordinance. Though there were no other motive in the world to move servants to obey their masters, yet their conscience toward God would move them. Such was Joseph’s manner of serving his master, as the reason which he himself gives to his mistress shows: “How could I do such a great evil and sin against God?”#footnote[Gen 39:9.] The prayer which Abraham’s servant made to God, and the thanks #footnote[Gen 24:12, 27.] which he gave him for blessing his journey, show that he served his master for the Lord’s sake. It is too plain to need proving that such was Jacob’s service to his master. This is all the more to be noted by Christian servants, because in this lies the greatest difference between #emph[believing servants] and others. Others may serve with fear and trembling, in singleness of heart, and with good will; but only saints do service as to Christ, for conscience’ sake. If this is not the thing they aim at #emph[only,] yet assuredly they aim at it #emph[chiefly] and #emph[principally.] This makes them not content themselves with doing the thing, but strive to do it in the best manner they can, so that God may best accept it. By this, as they approve themselves to God, so they do much good to their masters and bring much comfort to their own souls.

Contrary to this is the mind of most, who do all the service which #footnote[Service from ulterior motives.] they do from ulterior motives. They may perform much duty, and perhaps do much good to their masters; and on that account they may get good wages at their masters’ hands, and extraordinary rewards too, and live in much quiet under them; but they can look for no reward at God’s hands. So I may say to such servants what Christ said to those who did everything to have glory from men: “They already have their full reward.” #footnote[Matt 6:2.]

=== §. 23. #emph[Of servants’ willingness to perform their duty.]

The fourth branch concerning the #emph[manner] of servants’ performing #footnote[IIII.] their duty is noted in this phrase, #emph[with good will.] This #emph[good will] of a servant toward his master has respect partly to the disposition of the servant, and so it implies #emph[willingness] and cheerfulness; #footnote[Service to be done cheerfully.] and partly to the benefit of the master, and so it implies #emph[faithfulness.]

Of #emph[willingness] to do the duty which belongs to a servant, Christ Jesus (who took on himself “the form of a servant”)#footnote[Phil 2:7.] has made himself a worthy pattern. “I delight to do Your will,”#footnote[Ps 40:8.] he says to him who sent him; and again, “My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me and to finish His work.”#footnote[John 4:34.] Does not a man eat his food willingly, with delight and cheerfulness? Even so Christ did the work of him who sent him. So cheerfully did Jacob serve his uncle Laban that seven years “seemed but a few days” to him.#footnote[Gen 29:20.]

#emph[Objection.] The reason for that was #emph[the love he had for Rachel.]

1\. #emph[Answer.] This was #emph[one] reason, but not the #emph[only] reason. Had he not borne #emph[good will] to his uncle and master as well as #emph[love] to his wife, the time might have seemed tedious enough; but both meeting together made the time pass all the better.

2\. #emph[Answer.] If the love he had for Rachel made him do his service so cheerfully, then if servants love God, for whose sake they ought to do their service, it will be done cheerfully.

1\. What the apostle applies to the #emph[giving of alms] (2 Cor 9:7) may be extended to every kind of duty which God requires: #emph[God loves cheerfulness.] Therefore God has no regard for the work which is not seasoned with it.

2\. As #emph[cheerfulness] makes God like the work the better, so it makes the work much easier to him who does it. Our common proverb notes as much: #emph[Nothing is hard to a willing mind.]

3\. Let#footnote[#emph[Apostolus seruos monet dominis suis ex animo cum bona voluntate seruire, vt scilicet si non possunt à dominis liberi fieri, suam seruitutem ipsi quodammodo liberam faciant. Aug de Ciu. Dei, l.] 19. #emph[c.] 15. #emph[Direction.]] there be cheerfulness in a servant’s mind, and he is as free as his master: for such a servant is “the Lord’s freeman” (1 Cor 7:22); and when he cannot be made free by his master, he makes his service free, after a fashion.

Have an eye to God, to his acceptance and his reward, and it will put life into your spirit. Man’s reward makes poor men glad of work and cheerful in doing their work: it is like sugar in sour wine. Tradesmen, doctors, lawyers, all sorts of men are drawn on by gain to take great pains with great willingness. Should not God’s reward for our pains make us much more willing? Surely it would, if we had such an eye of #footnote[Heb 11:26.] faith as Moses had, to see by it the reward which he saw.

Contrary to this is heaviness of spirit and discontent of mind, #footnote[Contrary: discontent.] when servants do their service with a scowl, grudgingly, under compulsion (as bears are brought to the stake), and of necessity, like slaves in a galley. Such service is bound to be done badly; and even if it is done well outwardly, the doer can have little comfort in it, because God does not accept it.

=== §. 24. #emph[Of servants’ quickness and diligence in their service.]

Servants, as a testimony of their willingness and cheerfulness, must be both quick and diligent in their service, for these are effects of willingness. #emph[Quickness] has respect to the time of doing a thing; #emph[diligence,] to the pains that are taken over it.

He who is #emph[quick] in his service takes up no more time over #footnote[Quickness.] one thing than he must, but is ready to do one thing after another, and so does much more service. It is expressly noted of Rebekah that she #emph[hurried,]#footnote[Gen 24:18, 20.] and let down her jar; and #emph[hurried] and emptied her jar, and #emph[ran] back to the well. And Abraham’s servant, having made what speed he could to the place he was sent to, #emph[would not eat until he had told his errand.]#footnote[Gen 24:33.] After he had told it, if he had not succeeded, #footnote[#emph[v.] 49.] he would not have wasted time, but gone back at once; when he had carried out everything well, #footnote[#emph[v.] 56.] he would not linger over any courtesies, but hurried away to his master. Ahimaaz#footnote[2 Sam 18:23.] strove with the Cushite over which of them should be first to bring their message to their master; and Ahimaaz outran the Cushite, for he had a very willing mind to do the business. The kind of charge which Elisha gave to his servant implies all the speed he could make. It was this: “Tie up your garment … and go! If you meet anyone, do not greet him, and if anyone greets you, do not answer him.”#footnote[2 Kgs 4:29.]

He who is #emph[diligent] in his service will not only be quick in #footnote[Diligence.] time, but also keep at it, and employ all the labor and pains he can to carry out well what he has to do. That general charge laid on all men belongs in particular to a servant#footnote[Gen 3:19.] (“By the sweat of your brow you will eat your bread”), and so does what Solomon notes#footnote[Eccl 9:10.] (“Whatever you find to do with your hands, do it with all your might”). Great was Jacob’s diligence (for he says of himself, in doing his master’s business, “The heat consumed me by day and the frost by night, and sleep fled from my eyes”).#footnote[Gen 31:40.] Great also was the diligence of those shepherds who were “keeping watch over their flocks by night,”#footnote[Luke 2:8.] and of #footnote[Matt 25:16, 17.] those servants who by their pains doubled the talents which were committed to them. As diligence is commended to us by these and many similar examples, so it is further set forth by the many promises made to it, such as: “Diligent hands bring wealth”;#footnote[Prov 10:4.] “The work of a man’s hands returns to him”;#footnote[Prov 12:14.] “The hand of the diligent will rule”;#footnote[#emph[v.] 24.] #footnote[Prov 14:23.] “There is profit in all labor.”

The work which servants are appointed by their master to do is to them the work of God. That is why the apostle says to servants as well as to others, “Each one should lead the life … to which God has called him.”#footnote[1 Cor 7:17.] But the work of God is to be done with all diligence: for “Cursed is the one who is remiss in doing the work of the #smallcaps[Lord].”#footnote[Jer 48:10.]

Contrary to this are the idleness, laziness, slothfulness and sluggishness #footnote[Idleness.] of servants. These are faults far too common. Solomon speaks out much against these vices, describing them in their kind and setting down the many harms that follow from them.

He describes them like this: “The slacker says, ‘A lion is in the road!’”#footnote[Prov 26:13, 14, 15.] (that is, he claims some unlikely danger); “As a door turns on its hinges, so the slacker turns on his bed. The slacker buries his hand in the dish; it wearies him to bring it back to his mouth.” #footnote[Prov 24:33.] “A little sleep, a little slumber, a little folding of the hands to rest.”

These are the harms which he notes come from idleness and sloth: #emph[poverty,]#footnote[Prov 10:4.] #emph[servitude,]#footnote[Prov 12:24.] #emph[fruitless wishes and desires,]#footnote[Prov 13:4.] #emph[hunger,]#footnote[Prov 19:15.] #emph[beggary,]#footnote[Prov 20:4.] #emph[death.]#footnote[Prov 21:25.] And to show what little joy or comfort masters can have in lazy servants, he compares them to vinegar and smoke, which are as irritating to the teeth and eyes as anything can be: “Like vinegar”#footnote[Prov 10:26.] (he says) “to the teeth and smoke to the eyes, so is the slacker to those who send him.” And because many idlers think and say they do no harm, he further says that “whoever is slothful in his work is brother to him who is a great waster.”#footnote[Prov 18:9.] In this respect Christ calls a slothful servant #emph[a wicked]#footnote[Matt 25:26, 30.] and #emph[unprofitable servant,] and gives him the portion of those who do much harm.

However lightly many servants regard idleness and sloth, the truth is that it is plain theft. For the best service that servants can do is due to their master, and they ought to be as diligent in their master’s work as if it were their own. So it is not enough to avoid the idleness of doing nothing at all; they must also take care not to be slothful in doing something: for #emph[as good not to do it at all as to be never the better for it.]

I have pressed this point all the further because it is noted as a blemish in servants who profess religion that they are the laziest and most negligent, the least industrious and diligent. Much of the time which they should spend on their master’s business (which is their particular calling) they spend chattering about matters of state and church business (matters that do not belong to them). In this way they make their masters weary of their service; and because of this they are often shifted from house to house, and, like #emph[rolling stones,] they #emph[gather no moss.] They neither learn a skill by which they may maintain themselves and do good to others when they are on their own, nor lay up any stock or portion, as others who are diligent do. Indeed, they get such a habit of idleness that they can never shake it off again: for those who are slothful while under masters seldom turn out diligent when they work for themselves. In them Solomon’s proverb is borne out: “Mere talk leads only to poverty.” #footnote[Prov 14:23.]

=== §. 25. #emph[Of servants’ faithfulness.]

It was #footnote[§. 23.] noted before that the #emph[good will] here required of servants toward their masters had respect to the profit and benefit which servants might bring to their masters by their service; and in that respect it includes #emph[faithfulness] under it: #emph[faithfulness] (I say), by which servants discharge well the trust committed to them. Servants are expressly charged with “showing all good faithfulness,”#footnote[Titus 2:10.] and “it is required of stewards that they be found faithful.”#footnote[1 Cor 4:2.] The phrase which is used of Moses, “Moses was faithful as a servant,”#footnote[Heb 3:5.] shows that faithfulness belongs to a servant as, in a special way, properly his own; and #footnote[Matt 25:21.] where Christ couples these two, #emph[good] and faithful, he gives us to understand that a servant’s #emph[goodness] consists in his #emph[faithfulness.]

Great is the benefit that will come to both master and servant from servants’ faithfulness.

Solomon notes the benefit which comes to the master in this proverb: “Like the cold of snow” (which is very welcome, refreshing #footnote[Prov 25:13.] and profitable) “in the time of harvest” (when men are sweltering with heat) “is a faithful messenger to those who send him;#footnote[Prov 13:17.] he refreshes the soul of his masters”; and again in this: “A faithful envoy is health,” that is, he brings safety to his master.

Christ notes the benefit which comes to the servant himself by his faithfulness, in this approval and reward of it: “Well done, good and faithful servant! #footnote[Matt 25:21.] You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you in charge of many things. Enter into the joy of your master!” Every servant will be called to account: if not by his master on earth, yet by his master in heaven. He will say to every one, “Give an account of your stewardship.”#footnote[Luke 16:2.] Now then, if servants have not been faithful, what other settlement can they look for than what the wicked, slothful and unprofitable servant (who hid his talent in a cloth) received, namely this: “Throw that #footnote[Matt 25:30.] unprofitable servant into the outer darkness, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.” Let all unfaithful servants note this: for all unfaithfulness is contrary to faithfulness, such as negligence, deceit, thievery, treachery and similar vices. Much damage, disgrace and vexation is brought on masters by such servants; and it would have been better if they had never come into a man’s house.

But so that the point of faithfulness may be better discerned and observed, I will illustrate it in seven particulars in which it ought especially to be exercised. They are: 1. The #emph[goods,] 2. The #emph[business] and affairs, 3. The #emph[counsels] and secrets, 4. The other #emph[servants,] 5. The #emph[children,] 6. The #emph[spouse,] 7. The #emph[person] of their master or mistress.

=== §. 26. #emph[Of servants’ faithfulness with regard to their masters’ goods.]

Two things are required of servants to show their faithfulness with regard to their masters’ goods. #footnote[1.]

+ Keeping them safe.
+ Increasing them.

1\. Whatever masters commit to their servants, the servants must preserve so carefully that it is not lost, spoiled or impaired #footnote[Servants must preserve their masters’ goods well.] under their hands, whether the things are indoors or outdoors. To this purpose I may fittingly apply to servants the charge which the apostle gave to Timothy in another case: #footnote[1 Tim 6:20 τὴν παρακαταθήκην φύλαξον.] “Guard what has been entrusted to you.” The metaphor is taken from servants, and so it shows what their duty is. Great was Joseph’s faithfulness in this respect, so that his master “entrusted him with everything he owned.”#footnote[Gen 39:4.] So great was Jacob’s faithfulness that #footnote[Gen 31:38, 39.] in twenty years his master’s ewes and female goats, being under his hand, did not miscarry, nor were the rams devoured; if anything was torn by beasts, he did not bring it to his master, but bore the loss of it himself. If servants spot any damage or harm done by others to their masters’ goods, they must put it right if they can, or at least make it known to their masters, so that the master may give orders for putting it right; as did the servants of the man in the parable who sowed good seed, among which weeds were sown.#footnote[Matt 13:27.]

Masters who put servants in trust go about other affairs without worry, and do not look themselves to the goods which are in their servants’ keeping, as it is to be presumed they would if they did not trust their servants. There is great reason, therefore, for servants to be careful of the things so committed to them.

2\. They ought further to do what lies in them to advance #footnote[Servants must strive to increase their masters’ wealth. Gen 30:30. Matt 25:16, 17.] their master’s wealth and increase his stock. #emph[The little that Laban had was,] by Jacob’s faithfulness in this respect, #emph[increased many times over.] The talents which were committed to the two faithful servants were increased by their industry to as many more. So it is not enough for servants not to impair their master’s wealth; they must better it. For he who kept his master’s talent safe and gave him back his own was counted an unprofitable servant, and received the sentence of wasters.

Most masters take servants for their own advantage and benefit; for it is little relief to a master to provide food and lodging for many servants, and to give them wages, if he reaps no profit and benefit from them.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of servants’ carelessness over their masters’ goods.]

Contrary to servants’ #emph[faithfulness] in keeping safe their masters’ goods committed to their charge is #emph[carelessness] and negligence in allowing loss and damage to come to their masters. This happens in the fields when they do not look to his fences and gates, or neglect his cattle so that they are stolen, or allow diseases to grow on them, or do not give them fodder and water in due season. It happens in the house when they leave doors or windows open, and thieves come in and take away their masters’ goods, or hook them out; or carelessly leave the fire, or let candles burn so that the house may be set on fire; or allow household goods and clothing to lie until they are moth-eaten or otherwise spoiled; or allow anything in the house to be broken; or let food lie moldering in corners, or be thrown about for dogs and cats. When Christ so increased the bread and fish with which many thousands were fed that many fragments were left, he told his disciples to gather up what remained, and gave this reason: “so that nothing will be lost.”#footnote[John 6:12.] The damage which may come by servants’ carelessness may be more than they can ever make good; and therefore they ought to guard against it all the more carefully.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of servants’ fraud.]

Contrary to the other branch of servants’ #emph[faithfulness,] in increasing their masters’ wealth, is every kind of theft and fraud, whether by keeping back what is due to their masters or by stealing from them what they have. #footnote[Titus 2:10 μὴ νοσφιζομένους.] The apostle expressly forbids servants to #emph[purloin.] The word translated #emph[purloin] is the same by which the fraud of Ananias is set forth, who #footnote[Acts 5:2 ἐνοσφίσατο.] #emph[kept back] part of the price of a possession which they sold. So by it is forbidden not only open and manifest stealing, such as pilfering money out of their master’s purse, chest or counter, or carrying off his grain, wares or any other goods, but also putting more into the account of expenses than has been spent, or less into the account of debts than is due (like the unjust steward, who for a debt of a #emph[hundred] measures of oil put down #emph[fifty,] and for a #emph[hundred] of #footnote[Luke 16:6, 7.] wheat put down #emph[eighty]); or spending more than is needed; or bringing bad company into the house when their masters are absent, and treating them at their masters’ cost; or concealing part of the price taken for any wares; or borrowing money in their masters’ names; or taking greater fees than their masters know of; or receiving bribes or gifts which their masters refuse (as Gehazi did);#footnote[2 Kgs 5:22.] or hiring others to do their work at their masters’ cost; or enticing away their masters’ customers; or stealing other men’s goods committed to their masters’ trust (like tailors’ servants, who by this both greatly discredit their masters and also hinder their trade).

#emph[Objection.] What if masters withhold their servants’ wages? May the servants not right themselves by secret means?

#emph[Answer.] A master’s sin is no warrant for servants to answer sin with sin. The law is as open to servants as to masters; if not, God’s ears are open to their complaints: he can and will put all right, either here or hereafter. #footnote[Gen 31:42.] Note Jacob’s example. Many claim this as a just ground; but whether it is just or not, it is not safe to open this gap to deceit.

These and similar kinds of deceit are all the more heinous sins because of the trust which masters place in servants: for they violate both the bond of justice and the bond of faithfulness. If a servant embezzles from his master or defrauds him of any of his #footnote[21\. Of Hen. 8. Stat. 7. And 5. of Eliz. Stat. 10.] goods to the value of forty shillings, he is judged a felon by our statute law.

=== §. 29. #emph[Of servants’ faithfulness in the business which they are to carry out for their masters.]

In the business which masters commit to the care of #footnote[II.] their servants, to be carried out by them, servants must do their best so that all may prosper under their hands, as it is noted of Joseph, “he became a prosperous man.”#footnote[Gen 39:2.] We have not only a clear proof but also an excellent direction for this point in the #footnote[Gen 24.] example of Abraham’s servant, who was very faithful in carrying out the business of fetching a wife for Isaac. In it let these particulars be noted.

1\. He feared God: the whole conduct of that business testifies as much. There is a double bond to tie servants to this: one with respect to themselves, that in what they do they may be accepted by God; the other with respect to their masters, that their masters’ business may prosper under their hands. God prospers those who fear him in all they take in hand: witness the examples of Jacob,#footnote[Gen 30:27.] Joseph#footnote[Gen 39:2.] and David.#footnote[1 Sam 18:14.]

2\. He #footnote[Gen 24:12.] called on God to prosper his efforts. Now prayer is the best means that can possibly be used to obtain any blessing from the Lord. Without it all our efforts are in vain#footnote[Ps 127:2.] (#emph[it is in vain to rise early and stay up late, and to eat the bread of anxious toil, unless God’s blessing goes with it all]).

3\. He #footnote[Gen 24:27.] gave thanks when he saw the Lord beginning to prosper what he had taken in hand. Thanksgiving for the beginning of a blessing is an effectual means for the continuance of that blessing; indeed, it is also an effectual means to move God to prosper other things that we take in hand. So if servants desire to prosper in all the affairs which they undertake for their masters, they must give thanks for the first success which God has given, and so for the second, third, fourth, and for every success.

4\. He took every opportunity he could. #footnote[Gen 24:17, 23.] When he saw a young woman to inquire of, he ran to her and asked what was needful for his purpose. When he had ground to think she was the one, #footnote[#emph[v.] 22.] he presented gifts to her. #footnote[#emph[v.] 33.] As soon as he was brought to the house, he set about the matter for which he came, even before he ate anything. After matters were settled to his liking, he made no delay, but with all speed brought the young woman to Isaac. In a word, he omitted nothing that, to the utmost of his wisdom, he judged needful for that matter: he could have done no more if the matter had wholly concerned himself. I need urge this point no further than by setting this pattern before servants.

Contrary to this is the disposition of those who do not care whether the things which they do for their master prosper or not. If they have taken some pains over them, so that their master cannot say they have altogether neglected them, that is all they care about. If it does not succeed, they will say they are not in God’s place, able to make everything that is done prosper. But though the whole blessing rests in God, yet he has sanctified means for obtaining his blessing (such as faith, prayer, thanksgiving and the like); and because they do not use these, they fail in this point of faithfulness. Indeed, worse, they use the things which most hinder and keep away God’s blessing and bring a curse on all they do instead: such as profaneness, uncleanness, drunkenness and every kind of wild living; indeed, despising God’s word and holy ordinances, reproaching his saints, taking his name in vain by swearing, false swearing and blaspheming, lying, and also backbiting, pilfering and other similar vices. These irreligious and wicked servants, as they sin against God and their own souls, so they sin also against their master: it is not to a master’s profit to keep such servants.

=== §. 30. #emph[Of servants’ faithfulness in keeping their masters’ secrets, and concealing their infirmities.]

Because of the close bond between master #footnote[III.] and servants, and their close and continual living together, and the many tasks which masters have for their servants, servants come to know many of their masters’ secrets. #emph[Faithfulness] therefore requires them to keep these close. This holds provided that they are not such secrets as tend to the dishonor of God, or to the danger of the commonwealth and church, or even of a private person: for Jonathan#footnote[1 Sam 20:12.] is commended for revealing the harm which Saul had secretly intended against David.

To prove the point, note what Solomon says: “He who is of a faithful spirit keeps a matter hidden”#footnote[Prov 11:13.]: namely, a secret matter. Note also how faithful Jeremiah#footnote[Jer 38:27.] was in keeping Zedekiah’s counsel: though the officials asked about it, he would not reveal it to them.

To this head belongs a faithful concealing of masters’ infirmities: for the best people there are have many, and servants who are continually under their masters’ roof waiting on them cannot help noticing many. If servants are not faithful in this, masters would be better off without servants in their houses.

Contrary to keeping masters’ secrets close is blabbing about everything servants know concerning their masters. This is too common a fault: for when servants of different households, men or maids, meet together, nearly all their talk is of their masters and mistresses; and so it comes about that all the secrets of a house are soon known about the whole town or city. Solomon calls such a one a #emph[gossip:] “A gossip,” he says, “reveals a secret”; or, “He who #footnote[Prov 11:13; 20:19.] reveals secrets is a gossip”: for many of Solomon’s proverbs are convertible; they can be turned either way. Now note how Solomon shows the harm that gossips do: “The words of a gossip are like wounds” (they wound a man’s precious name and reputation) “that go down into the inmost #footnote[Prov 18:8.] being,” that is, they strike, as it were, through the very heart of a man. Again, he notes that they are the cause of all strife and stir up discord between #emph[close friends;]#footnote[Prov 16:28. Prov 26:20.] and in that respect he fitly likens them to #emph[wood,] which is the very fuel of fire. What enemies, then, such servants are to a house! They are like treacherous spies, the most dangerous enemies there are. So we see that this is no light sin; yet it is all the more odious when the infirmities of masters and mistresses (the publishing of which may greatly damage their reputation) are made known.

=== §. 31. #emph[Of servants’ faithfulness in helping one another.]

Where many fellow servants are together, #emph[faithfulness] requires that one be helpful to another in what they can, #footnote[IV.] as by good example, good counsel, encouragement in good courses, dissuasion from vile and wicked practices, peace and unity, and the like. The Lord Christ expressly calls such a one a #emph[faithful servant,] and pronounces him #emph[blessed.] #footnote[Matt 24:45, 46.]

The example and advice of an equal prevails much with another, so that a fellow servant may in this way do more good than the master himself. And if by his means he brings his fellow servants to be faithful, his own faithfulness is doubled and trebled, and his master receives a double and treble benefit by it: namely, the benefit of this good servant’s faithfulness, and the benefit of the faithfulness of all the other servants whom he has made faithful.

Contrary to this is the practice of those who by their bad example corrupt #footnote[Matt 24:49.] their fellows (like that evil servant who, when his master was away, #emph[ate and drank with the drunken,] that is, caused others to be drunk with him); or by bad counsel draw one another on to evil (like the sons of Jacob, who, when they saw Joseph far off, said to one another, “Here comes that dreamer!#footnote[Gen 37:18, #emph[etc.]] Come now, let us kill him,” etc.; and like those who, when they saw their master’s son,#footnote[Matt 21:38.] said among themselves, “This is the heir. Come, let us kill him and take his inheritance”); or are always quarreling with their fellows and striking them (like that evil servant named above, whom the Lord threatens to cut off); or dissuade one another from obedience and subjection (like those who said, “Let us break Their chains #footnote[Ps 2:3.] and cast away Their cords”; and like Sheba, who said, “We have no share in David … Every man to his tent, O Israel!”). #footnote[2 Sam 20:1.] These faults are very common among servants; and so it comes about that there are so few good servants and so many bad. This proverb is all too true: #emph[One scabby sheep spoils a whole flock.] Let there be in a large family one servant who is profane, proud, dissolute, defiant, rebellious or otherwise vicious, and all will soon be like him.

Among other parts of unfaithfulness of this kind, one of the most monstrous (which is yet far too frequent) is to entice one another to uncleanness, and for men and maids to defile one another. We showed #footnote[§. 11.] before that it was unlawful for servants to marry during the time of their service without their master’s consent: how abominable, then, it is to defile one another! The sin is doubled when it is between servants: for as it is a beastly sin in itself, so, in the respect just named, it greatly dishonors their master and his house. Besides this, the maid so defiled is often unable to do her service well; indeed, many times the charge of the child falls on the master. So shame and dishonor, grief and vexation, loss and damage all meet together, the more to gall and pierce him to the very heart. Is not this, then, a great part of unfaithfulness? Does it not deserve to be severely punished, and that openly and publicly, with shame and pain too, so that others may take warning by it? Many use means to escape the avenging hand of man; but though they escape man’s hand, they will be sure to meet with God’s heavy vengeance: “God will judge the sexually immoral.” Daily experience shows what misery #footnote[Heb 13:4.] such wretches bring on themselves, and how God catches up with them, and that most fearfully.

=== §. 32. #emph[Of servants’ faithfulness with regard to their masters’ children.]

Servants may show great faithfulness to their masters #footnote[V.] in their dealings with their children: such as, while the children are young and not able to look after themselves, to be tender toward them and tend them well; maids especially to handle them neatly and look after them, and to bring them up cleanly; and those who have a particular charge of them, to seek their good in everything they can and give them their due portion; and (because children are much in servants’ company) to use such speech in their hearing as may minister grace to them, and to teach them good things; and (when they grow to some ripeness of years) to bear a reverent respect toward them and esteem them their betters, because they are their master’s children. It is noted of Abraham’s servant that he called his master’s #footnote[Gen 24:65.] son #emph[master.]

Children are dear to parents: they count the honor and kindness done to them as done to themselves. This kind of faithfulness must therefore be highly valued by masters in their servants; and assuredly it is a great means of working a good regard in masters toward them.

Yet many servants behave #emph[contrary to this] toward #footnote[Points of unfaithfulness in servants toward their masters’ children.] their masters’ children, as is evident from these particulars.

1\. Some are very boorish and churlish in their conduct toward their masters’ children when the parents are out of sight.

2\. Others are very careless of them, and tend them in a very slovenly way, not caring how they look; by which they often bring great displeasure on themselves from their master and mistress.

3\. Others get what they can from their masters’ children, and deprive them of their allowance, turning it to their own gain.

4\. Others greatly corrupt their masters’ children with their filthy and corrupt talk, teaching them to swear, blaspheme and use every kind of unclean speech. So that proverb is borne out: “Evil talk #footnote[1 Cor 15:33.] corrupts good character.” Children often learn in their young years such bad language and behavior from wicked servants that their parents can never get them to leave it again; so that they may curse the day that such servants ever came into their house.

5\. Others entice them to stage-plays, to gaming houses and other such places, which are the very bane of youth, and draw them to spend in wild living the allowance their parents give them, indeed beyond that allowance, so that they run into debt and get such a habit of spending that at length they squander their whole estate.

6\. Others entice their affections, and often draw them into folly and uncleanness. This filthy kind of unfaithfulness is as much worse than that which was #footnote[§. 31.] noted before between fellow servants as children are nearer and dearer to their parents than servants are to their masters.

7\. Others, who dare not commit this abominable wickedness, do not hesitate to do what is little better: namely, to draw them on to be betrothed, indeed often married, to them, and that secretly, without their parents’ consent. By this, parents’ affections are often so alienated from their children that they will not acknowledge them as children, but cast them off completely. These are the fruits of this wicked kind of unfaithfulness in servants.

=== §. 33. #emph[Of servants’ faithfulness with regard to their master’s or mistress’s spouse.]

Servants ought to be so faithful to their masters and #footnote[VI.] mistresses that, if one of them should try to use a servant in any kind of deceit against the other, the servant ought not to yield. For example, a master might urge his maid secretly to take away jewels, silver plate, money, linen or any such thing as is in her mistress’s keeping. It makes no difference that the master has the chief power over all the goods: a secret taking of them away without the knowledge of the mistress in whose keeping they are is deceit in the servant, and a point of unfaithfulness. Much less ought any servants to be moved by their mistress secretly to take away their master’s grain, wares or any goods for her private use. Of the two, this is the greater part of unfaithfulness.

If such deceit ought not to be used about any goods, much less about the body of master or mistress. For example, if a master should entice his maid to commit folly with him, or a mistress her manservant, both their conscience toward God and their faithfulness to their master or mistress should make them utterly refuse it, and give no place to any such temptation. Joseph#footnote[Gen 39:7. #emph[etc.]] is set before us as a pattern in this; and against his mistress’s urging he gives the two reasons named above. He shows his conscience toward God in these words: “How could I do such a great evil and sin against God?” He shows his faithfulness to his master in these: “He has withheld nothing from me except you … So how could I,” etc.

To this head may be referred servants’ faithfulness in making known to their master the sin of his wife, and to their mistress the sin of her husband, especially if it is such a sin as may tend to the ruin of the family, and so that, by knowing of it, the party who is not blinded and besotted with the sin, but rather free from it, may be a means to redress it. In this way Nabal’s#footnote[1 Sam 25:14.] servants made known to Abigail Nabal’s churlishness toward David’s servants, by which means the harm intended against the house was averted. So if servants know that their master intends some deadly revenge against another, to tell his wife of it in time may be great faithfulness; or if they know that their mistress has planned to go away secretly from her husband, to tell him of it is a part of faithfulness. This may be applied to many other similar cases.

The contrary is yielding to masters or mistresses in any point of deceit against one another. Servants are too prone to this, because they think they will be shielded by the authority of the party who sets them to work to deceive. But no authority can be a warrant for any deceit or wickedness.

=== §. 34. #emph[Of servants’ faithfulness with regard to their masters’ persons.]

Masters and mistresses are flesh and blood as well as servants, #footnote[VII.] and so subject to weakness, sickness, old age and other distresses, in which they may stand in great need of servants’ help. Servants therefore must be faithful in giving them the best help they can. Saul’s servants did a part of faithfulness to their master when, as he was tormented by an evil spirit, #footnote[1 Sam 16:16.] they inquired after means to ease him. So did David’s servants when, as he was bedridden, #footnote[1 Kgs 1:2.] they sought out one to care for him. It was a point of faithfulness in Naaman’s #footnote[2 Kgs 5:3.] maid to tell her mistress of a means by which her master might be cured of his leprosy; and in his #footnote[#emph[v.] 13.] servants, to persuade him to use the means prescribed by the prophet.

Contrary to this is a servant’s ungrateful and inhumane leaving of his master in his time of need, as the servants of Job did. For when the hand of God lay heavy on him, and all his goods were taken from him, and his body was full of sore boils, #emph[those who dwelt in his house, and his maids, counted him a stranger; he was a foreigner in their sight; he called his servant, and he gave him no answer.]#footnote[Job 19:15, 16.] So Ziba#footnote[2 Sam 19:26.] left Mephibosheth in his greatest need; and so #footnote[Matt 26:56.] the disciples fled from their master Jesus Christ. But what shall we say of those who take occasion from their master’s helplessness to murder him themselves, as Rechab#footnote[2 Sam 4:6.] and Baanah did; or to betray him to his enemies, as Judas#footnote[Matt 26:15.] betrayed the Lord Christ? Only this: that such treacherous servants may look for such ends.
